Keep the entire Suhail app unchanged.

Modify ONLY:
1. the indoor floor map structure and spatial clarity
2. the active navigation experience

Do NOT change:
- the bottom navigation
- the Ask Suhail button
- the search bar
- the color palette
- the Schedule screen
- the Game screen
- the Profile screen
- the appointment flow
- the overall app structure or branding

==================================================
PART 1 — FIX THE INDOOR MAP
==================================================

The current indoor map looks like disconnected room cards floating on a background.

Redesign the map so it clearly reads as a REAL university building with understandable circulation.

VERY IMPORTANT:
The user must immediately understand:
- this is a corridor
- this is a room
- this is a wall
- this is a door
- this is where I walk
- this is where the stairs and elevator are

Redesign the floor plan with:

- wide, continuous corridors connecting all rooms
- a clear main central corridor
- smaller secondary corridors branching from it
- clearly defined walls
- visible room entrances / doors opening into corridors
- realistic spacing and proportions between rooms
- realistic university layout logic
- stairs and elevator physically integrated into the building circulation
- proper connection between classrooms, lecture hall, study area, faculty offices, and student lounge

Use clear architectural hierarchy:

ROOMS:
- slightly raised 2.5D / isometric room volumes
- soft shadows
- clear labels

CORRIDORS:
- wider and visually obvious
- clearly different floor material/color from rooms
- continuous and unobstructed
- one of the clearest visual elements of the map

DOORS:
- visible openings between rooms and corridors

STAIRS / ELEVATOR:
- built into the plan, not floating like UI buttons
- use clear stair/elevator symbols

Make the map resemble a premium modern indoor campus navigation map, similar in feel to Concept3D.

Do NOT arrange rooms like UI cards.

Do NOT leave large empty meaningless spaces.

The floor plan should make sense as a BUILDING first, then as a beautiful app map.

==================================================
PART 2 — TWO VISUAL MODES
==================================================

The app must use TWO different visual modes:

1. BROWSING MODE
- Used when exploring the building, searching, or selecting a destination
- Keep this mode as a polished 2.5D / isometric indoor university map
- This is the normal map mode

2. ACTIVE NAVIGATION MODE
- When the user presses “Start Navigation”, switch into a FIRST-PERSON indoor walking perspective
- This first-person perspective must become the main visual experience during navigation
- The user should feel like they are walking through a real university building

==================================================
PART 3 — FIRST-PERSON NAVIGATION
==================================================

When active navigation begins, do NOT keep the top-down map as the main view.

Instead, show a FIRST-PERSON indoor corridor view from normal human eye level.

The user should appear to be walking inside a university building.

The first-person environment should include:
- university hallways
- classroom doors
- room numbers
- corridor turns
- stairs and elevators
- lobby / study area transitions
- signage and directional cues
- destination indicators

Examples of instruction overlays:
- “Continue straight — 25 m”
- “Turn right after the study area”
- “Take the stairs to Floor 2”
- “B203 is on your right”

During active navigation, show:
- first-person corridor environment as the main visual
- a directional arrow overlay
- current instruction
- remaining distance
- estimated walking time
- destination label
- an optional small mini-map as a secondary aid only

The mini-map must stay small and secondary.
The first-person view must remain dominant.

Do NOT make active navigation look like:
- car navigation
- city navigation
- a top-down floor plan
- a third-person game
- an architectural blueprint

==================================================
PART 4 — TRANSITIONS
==================================================

The flow should work like this:

2.5D indoor map browsing mode
→ search for B203
→ select B203
→ tap “Start Navigation”
→ smooth transition into first-person indoor walking view
→ follow corridor directions
→ if needed, show floor transition
→ continue first-person walking view
→ arrive at B203
→ show check-in / arrival / +50 points

The transition between map mode and first-person mode should feel smooth, premium, and suitable for a hackathon demo video.

==================================================
PART 5 — FINAL QUALITY
==================================================

The result must clearly communicate:

- browsing and search happen in a clear 2.5D indoor map
- actual navigation happens in a first-person walking view
- the indoor map has clear circulation and realistic architecture
- the user feels guided through a real university building
- the experience is immersive, polished, and visually strong

IMPORTANT:
Do not redesign the rest of the application.
Only improve the indoor map clarity and active navigation experience.